      "text": "<think>\nTo find the product of 99 and 31, I'll start by breaking down the multiplication into more manageable parts.\n\nFirst, I'll express 99 as (100 - 1). This allows me to use the distributive property effectively.\n\nNext, I'll multiply 31 by both 100 and -1 separately. Multiplying by 100 is straightforward, and then I'll adjust for the negative sign.\n\nAfter calculating both parts, I'll add them together to get the final result.\n</think>\n\nTo find the product of **99** and **31**, follow these steps:\n\n1. **Break Down the Numbers:**\n   \n   - **99** can be written as **(100 - 1)**.\n   - **31** remains as it is.\n\n2. **Apply the Distributive Property:**\n   \n   \\[\n   99 \\times 31 = (100 - 1) \\times 31\n   \\]\n\n3. **Multiply Each Term:**\n   \n   \\[\n   = 100 \\times 31 - 1 \\times 31\n   \\]\n   \n   \\[\n   = 3100 - 31\n   \\]\n\n4. **Subtract the Results:**\n   \n   \\[\n   = 3100 - 31 = 3069\n   \\]\n\n**Final Answer:**\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{3069}\n\\]",
